id: pm-rust-5tey
title: Create canonical items with crash-safe native Rust transactions
description: Add the first Rust-native write surface as an explicit-id create transaction that is compatible with pm 2026.8.6 item and history formats and remains safe under concurrent processes and injected failures.
type: Feature
status: closed
priority: 1
tags: []
created_at: "2026-08-07T10:07:20.525Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-07T11:23:41.193Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-07T11:02:58.500Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-07T11:02:58.500Z"
author: codex
estimated_minutes: 720
acceptance_criteria: A Rust SDK API and CLI create canonical TOON items with explicit IDs and no JavaScript Node Bun or shell bridge; Per-item exclusive locks reject concurrent duplicate writers without overwriting item or history bytes; Atomic item writes and a durable transaction journal recover every injected interruption between item and history commits; History JSONL patch and hashes differentially match pm 2026.8.6 for the supported create contract; Unit integration property multi-process black-box and real installed-pm acceptance pass at exact 100 percent line function region and branch coverage
definition_of_ready: The read-only slice pm-rust-o2yr is closed and canonical pm 2026.8.6 contracts are available read-only
value: Lets native agents create durable work without starting a JavaScript runtime
impact: Establishes the transaction and locking primitives required by every later mutation
outcome: A minimal but production-safe native create operation interoperable with canonical pm workspaces
why_now: Read compatibility is proven; mutation safety is the next critical vertical boundary
parent: pm-rust-u4al
reviewer: maintainer
risk: high
confidence: medium
sprint: 2026-08-native-mutations
release: 0.1.0-alpha.2
resolution: "Delivered a Rust-only explicit-ID create transaction with canonical pm 2026.8.6 item/history bytes, durable recovery, ownership-safe locking, and exact quality evidence."
expected_result: "Rust SDK and CLI creation is interoperable, race-safe, crash-recoverable, and fully covered without a JavaScript runtime bridge."
actual_result: "The SDK and CLI create every built-in item type; exact official fixtures and live pm 2026.8.6 reads pass; simultaneous writers preserve one complete result; all recovery states, no-replace publication, and filesystem failures pass; 36 tests achieve exact 100/100/100/100 coverage and cargo audit is clean."
notes[4]:
  - created_at: "2026-08-07T11:02:56.834Z"
    author: codex
    text: "Verification: 36 tests pass across unit, property, SDK, CLI, multi-process concurrency, lock replacement, atomic no-replace publication, crash recovery, and real filesystem failure suites; cargo-llvm-cov reports exact 100 percent lines/functions/regions/branches; cargo audit found no vulnerabilities; official pm 2026.8.6 read, history, health, and validate accepted a Rust-created item."
    edited_at: "2026-08-07T11:06:25.108Z"
  - created_at: "2026-08-07T11:12:26.701Z"
    author: codex
    text: "Exact-head PR CI repair: Windows now performs a real fallible target sync instead of compiling a no-op Result helper; fresh CI clones install their clone-local pm merge drivers before the read-only health gate. The lock-replacement test now uses a real Linux FIFO recovery barrier, eliminating the timing race while exercising the same ownership invariant."
  - created_at: "2026-08-07T11:18:03.899Z"
    author: codex
    text: "Exact-head CI follow-up: Windows exposed that durability syncing a published file and syncing a directory after deletion are distinct operations. The transaction now syncs the published target on non-Unix platforms, syncs parent directories after publication/deletion on Unix, and never reopens a deleted Windows journal or lock. Reproducing the CI nightly coverage command also revealed one untested valid numeric-ID branch; a behavioral create regression now covers it, restoring measured 100/100/100/100 (864 lines, 102 functions, 1272 regions, 130 branches). The CI gate now prints all four totals before enforcing the unchanged exact threshold."
  - created_at: "2026-08-07T11:23:41.193Z"
    author: codex
    text: "Second exact-head Windows/TDD finding: Windows requires the original temporary-file handle to be closed before unlinking its private hard-link name; publication now writes and fsyncs, atomically hard-links without replacement, explicitly closes the handle, removes the private name, then applies platform durability. A repeated property run also shrank a TOON incompatibility to description = -A; list-like leading-hyphen scalars now remain quoted and have a permanent explicit round-trip regression in addition to the property suite."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-07T11:02:57.158Z",codex,"Property generation exposed that generic scalar dequoting must never be applied to TOON array lines: the Rust encoder correctly quoted the string tag 0, but normalization could remove it. Preserve non-empty tag arrays byte-for-byte and normalize only canonical empty-array syntax."
